REGRESSION I SHIPPED, and why my test could not have caught it (line 14).
Last pass I moved the option-wheel's scroll handlers from the track onto the
individual cards, reasoning that a card's transformed box is exactly the
pixels the cursor can see. That broke scrolling entirely: 'The ENTIRE page is
scrolling down when I just try to scroll the wheel.'

Wheel gestures are LATCHED. The browser picks a scroll target when the gesture
begins and sends the rest of the gesture there. The cards are transform-animated
and scale down as they leave centre, so the moment the wheel turns, the element
under the cursor slides out from under it; one missed event hands the whole
remaining gesture to the document.

RULE: a scroll-capture region must not be the thing that moves.

My verification was also structurally incapable of finding it — a single
mouse.wheel() call is ONE event, and latching only exists across a multi-event
gesture. Testing a gesture-based behaviour with a single event tests nothing.
Generalised: match the granularity of the test to the granularity of the
mechanism. Now checked with 4-5 successive events at three cursor positions.

